ich no HABS sheet draws","the absence of dormers on the Prairie Avenue roof slope"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"Wheeler, George H., Residence (1812 S. Prairie Ave.). Albert Levy, Architectural Photographic Series (New York), nos. 31, 33; photograph of the street front, c. 1885-1900. Ryerson & Burnham Art and Architecture Archives, Art Institute of Chicago, Special 779.4 L66a no. 31, 33.","source_id":"levy_1812_prairie_photo","tier":1,"tier_label":"period/eyewitness","url":"https://artic.contentdm.oclc.org/digital/collection/mqc/id/13492","what_it_does_not_supply":["colour: the print is monochrome, so every stone, brick and paint colour stays reconstructed","the side and rear walls, and the roof behind the gable except its two front corners","depth: how far the projection stands out is the 1911 sheet's"],"what_it_supplies":["the front's storey heights, the raised basement and the stair's rise, read at the front's plane","the south projection's width and height and its windows","the entrance's place on the north and the openings' places and sizes","the shaped gable's outline, its grid window and finial"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"Robinson, E. Robinson's Atlas of the City of Chicago. New York: E. Robinson, 1886. Plate 10, \"Part of 2.39.14 Assessors Div.\"","source_id":"robinson_1886_chicago_plate_10","tier":2,"tier_label":"near-primary recollection","url":"","what_it_does_not_supply":["Street widths: its bands are not drawn to the widths it prints (Prairie printed 66, drawn about 55 ft)","Anything as of 1904 on its own authority","Elevations or grades"],"what_it_supplies":["The water's edge beside the Illinois Central from above 16th Street to 18th, as of 1886 -- a bound, from before, on the 1904 edge","Lot and subdivision lines, and the outlines of the houses at 1609-1611 and 1620 Prairie that Glessner House names as standing in 1904 and gone by 1911"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"Sanborn Map Company. Insurance Maps of Chicago, Illinois, Volume 3. New York: Sanborn Map Company, 1911. Sheet 20.","source_id":"sanborn_1911_chicago_v3_sheet_20","tier":1,"tier_label":"period/eyewitness","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/sanborn01790_020/","what_it_does_not_supply":["Anything as of 1904 on its own authority. It is a 1911 survey; Glessner House's reading that everything on sheets 20, 28 and 35 but 1609-1611 and 1620 Prairie stood in 1904 is attributed guidance, recorded in that README, and not checked by this record","A construction date for any building it draws","Elevations, grades, curb heights or fill depths -- no Sanborn sheet carries a level","Architecture above the footprint beyond its story count, material colour and roof symbol","The lake edge beyond the stretch the sheet actually inks: the pen line stops while the tracks run on"],"what_it_supplies":["The Illinois Central / Michigan Central right-of-way and, from the 16th Street row south about 60 m, the water's edge beside it, as of 1911","Footprints, lot lines, street widths (66 ft; 16th Street 50 ft), addresses, material by colour and story counts for Indiana Av. to the tracks, 16th to 18th Street, as of 1911","Its own printed Scale of Feet, which the georeference uses as its scale"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"Sanborn Map Company. Insurance Maps of Chicago, Illinois, Volume 3. New York: Sanborn Map Company, 1911. Sheet 28.","source_id":"sanborn_1911_chicago_v3_sheet_28","tier":1,"tier_label":"period/eyewitness","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/sanborn01790_020/","what_it_does_not_supply":["Anything as of 1904 on its own authority. It is a 1911 survey; Glessner House's reading that everything on sheets 20, 28 and 35 but 1609-1611 and 1620 Prairie stood in 1904 is attributed guidance, recorded in that README, and not checked by this record","A construction date for any building it draws","Elevations, grades, curb heights or fill depths -- no Sanborn sheet carries a level","Architecture above the footprint beyond its story count, material colour and roof symbol","The lake edge beyond the stretch the sheet actually inks: the pen line stops while the tracks run on"],"what_it_supplies":["The water's edge beside the Illinois Central from about 40 m north of 18th Street to about 80 m south of it, and the easternmost track on to the paper's edge, as of 1911","The 1800 Prairie lot and the Glessner House footprint (stone, courtyard plan) as of 1911, and every Prairie- and Calumet-facing footprint from 18th to 20th Street","Its own printed Scale of Feet"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"Sanborn Map Company. Insurance Maps of Chicago, Illinois, Vol. 3 (1911), sheet 28: E. 18th St. to E. 20th St., Prairie Av. Blvd. and Calumet Av. to the Illinois Central / Michigan Central tracks and Lake Michigan. Scale 1\" = 50'. Stamped 'Fire Insurance Map Collection, Geography and Map Division, Library of Congress'; header 'Chicago Vol. 3', with 1891-edition cross-references (245-246) (252-253).","source_id":"sanborn_1911_vol3_sheet_28","tier":1,"tier_label":"period/eyewitness","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/sanborn01790_020/","what_it_does_not_supply":["anything about 1904 directly: a 1911 sheet shows only that a structure stood in 1911","the 1904 use of the west wing: '1911 GARAGE' cannot be backdated","architecture above the footprint - roof pitches, chimneys, openings","lot dimensions in figures for this lot: none are printed on it"],"what_it_supplies":["the house's position on its lot: north face on the E. 18th Street line, west face on the alley line, east face about 14.7 ft (±1.5 ft) behind the Prairie Avenue street line","a check on the HABS envelope: about 161 x 75 ft overall and a 35 x 59 ft west wing at 1\" = 50'","materials by colour (stone street walls, brick courtyard walls) and storeys (2B main house, 1½ west wing) in 1911","the neighbour that closes the courtyard on the south in 1911 (1808 Prairie and its rear garage)"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"J. W. Taylor (Chicago), 'Kimball, William W., Residence (1801 S. Prairie Ave.)', photograph no. 19825, c. 1895. Art Institute of Chicago, Ryerson & Burnham Art and Architecture Archives, Historic Architecture and Landscape Image Collection (Architecture Photograph Collection), MPDrawer 5; gift of Thomas E. Tallmadge, 1918.","source_id":"taylor_c1895_kimball_residence_1801","tier":1,"tier_label":"period/eyewitness","url":"https://artic.contentdm.oclc.org/digital/collection/mqc/id/28957","what_it_does_not_supply":["the plan: the 1911 Sanborn sheet 28's trace controls it","the south and east sides and the rear, which the camera does not see","colour (monochrome print); 'Bedford stone' is the register's reading","anything changed between c. 1895 and 1 July 1904"],"what_it_supplies":["the massing as built: the front, the flank and the east range, their storeys and heights at the photograph's scale","the round tower's place on the 18th Street flank (not at the corner), its girth, its eaves and the height of its cone","the steep slate roof over the main block, its deck and cresting, its dormers","the south pavilion's stone gable, the bowed bay and the corner loggia with its arches and stair","the east turret at the far end of the flank"]}],"content_version":420257878,"default_mode":"walk","endings":[{"completion_eligible":true,"id":"finish","read_s":10,"text":"End of the walk so far. As more buildings are built out they join it, in their place by detail and area."}],"evidence":[{"confidence":"reconstructed","id":"tour","liberty":"L-jaunt-prairie-built-so-far","sources":[],"text":"Which buildings the walk includes, their detail levels, its grouping and order, reading times and keepsake are this project's own description of its models, rewritten from the records by tools/jaunt_built_so_far.py; no historical visit is asserted."},{"confidence":"attested","id":"stood-1","locator":"HABS ILL-1015 data pages 13-15 (PDF pp. 14-16): roofs, walls, courtyard elevation, adjoining-house brick, iron-work, windows, the narrow north windows along 18th Street; p. 9 (PDF p. 10): the courtyard entrance from the alley, the Virginia creeper on the courtyard walls and the Boston ivy on the front; p. 15 (PDF p. 16): the service entrance through the great arch on Eighteenth Street.","sources":["glessner_story_of_a_house_1923","habs_glessner_house_il_1015_data_pages","inland_architect_1888_02_glessner_exterior"],"text":"Glessner House, 1800 Prairie stood on 1 July 1904, as its structure record carries it."},{"confidence":"inferred","id":"stood-2","reasoning":"STANDING IN 1904 is what the scene needs, and it rests on the 1911 Sanborn sheet 28 carried back seven years, the same reading the Glessner record and the 1904 parcels make of it (the Glessner House correspondent reads no change on this sheet between 1904 and 1911). FROM: the T-1837 building register reports 1886 (Cobb & Frost); it is not a source record here, so the day is the 1st of January and the confidence inferred. TO: the register reports the loss in 1968; same footing, the year's last day.","sources":["sanborn_1911_vol3_sheet_28","sanborn_1911_chicago_v3_sheet_28"],"text":"Osborn Keith House, 1808 Prairie stood on 1 July 1904, as its structure record carries it."},{"confidence":"inferred","id":"stood-3","reasoning":"The draft claims the 1904 target and no more: the 1911 sheet draws the building and the T-1840 census rules it present in 1904 (present_as_mapped). THE HOUSE'S DATE AND ITS MANSARD'S (T-2962): the house was 'completed in 1864' for Granville S. Ingraham (glessner_blog_2020_07_early_history_prairie_iv), and that post's author judges the mansard 'may well have been a later addition' to an Italianate house of the Civil War years; nothing read here dates the mansard, and the Keith house at 1900 Prairie had its mansard added in the 1880s (glessner_blog_2014_11_elbridge_keith_house), so a later mansard is the street's own pattern, not a proof for this house. For this phase the question is bounded either way: the c. 1890 photograph (earliest 1885) shows the mansard and the veranda front, and the 1911 sheet draws the mansard's curb and prints the house '3B', so the house as built here stood from at least the late 1880s to 1911. The range keeps to the 1904 target because only the front is seen in c. 1890; the flank's bays are the 1911 sheet's. When the house lost the mansard or was demolished is not read.","sources":["sanborn_1911_chicago_v3_sheet_20","glessner_blog_1736_mcbirney_c1890_photo","glessner_blog_2020_07_early_history_prairie_iv"],"text":"1736 Prairie stood on 1 July 1904, as its structure record carries it."},{"confidence":"inferred","id":"stood-4","reasoning":"Built for George H. Wheeler on the Real Estate and Building Journal's permit of June 7, 1884 ('3-story dwelling, 30x80', the Prairie library's npo-1812-rebj-1884-06-07-wheeler-dwelling-permit), photographed by Albert Levy c. 1885-1900 and drawn on the 1911 sheet ('3B'); the T-1841 census rules it present in 1904 (present_as_mapped). The range claims the 1904 target and the sheet's date and no more.","sources":["sanborn_1911_chicago_v3_sheet_28","levy_1812_prairie_photo"],"text":"Wheeler House, 1812 Prairie stood on 1 July 1904, as its structure record carries it."},{"confidence":"inferred","id":"stood-5","reasoning":"The 1904 target to the 1911 sheet that draws the house: the T-1841 census rules it present in 1904 (present_as_mapped), and the photograph (after 1873) shows the same plan. Its building and loss dates are not read here.","sources":["sanborn_1911_chicago_v3_sheet_28"],"text":"Henderson House, 1816 Prairie stood on 1 July 1904, as its structure record carries it."},{"confidence":"inferred","id":"stood-6","reasoning":"The draft claims the 1904 target and no more: the 1911 sheet draws the building and the T-1841 census rules it present in 1904 (present_as_mapped). Its building and loss dates are the per-building ticket's to read.","sources":["sanborn_1911_chicago_v3_sheet_28"],"text":"Coleman-Ames House, 1811 Prairie stood on 1 July 1904, as its structur