+{"allowed_modes":["walk","fly","instantly"],"category":"Build log","citations":[{"archived_url":"","citation":"[The Ingraham/McBirney house, 1736 S. Prairie Avenue, as it appeared circa 1890.] Photograph from the east side of Prairie Avenue looking west across the street; photographer and original holder not named. Reproduced by the Glessner House Museum, 'The Early History of Prairie Avenue - Part IV', The Story of a House (blog), 7 July 2020.","source_id":"glessner_blog_1736_mcbirney_c1890_photo","tier":1,"tier_label":"period/eyewitness","url":"https://glessnerhouse.blogspot.com/2020/07/the-early-history-of-prairie-avenue.html","what_it_does_not_supply":["the 1904 state: the photograph is c. 1890","colour: the photograph is monochrome","the south flank on 18th Street, the north wall, the rear and the roof behind the street face","the principal-storey windows' heads, hidden under the veranda's frieze"],"what_it_supplies":["the veranda across the front: its four posts, balustrade, frieze and roof, and the stair at its north end","three upper-storey windows and their heights, arched under eared hoods","the bracketed cornice's height, about 9.9 m over grade through its camera","the mansard, its curb about 12.7 m over grade, and its two street dormers","the principal floor about 1.85 m over grade (the veranda's deck)"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"Glessner House Museum, 'The Early History of Prairie Avenue - Part IV', The Story of a House (blog), 7 July 2020.","source_id":"glessner_blog_2020_07_early_history_prairie_iv","tier":4,"tier_label":"later scholarship","url":"https://glessnerhouse.blogspot.com/2020/07/the-early-history-of-prairie-avenue.html","what_it_does_not_supply":["a date for the mansard","measurement","anything of the flank, the rear or the alley building"],"what_it_supplies":["that 1736 was a large brick house completed in 1864 for Granville S. Ingraham","the author's judgement that its mansard may well have been a later addition to an Italianate house","that Hugh McBirney lived in it for many years"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"Glessner, John J. The Story of a House. Unpublished typescript booklet written for his children George and Frances, signed 'Faithfully your father, John J. Glessner', March 1923. Transcribed in full in HABS No. ILL-1015, Written Historical and Descriptive Data, data pages 7-21 (1963).","source_id":"glessner_story_of_a_house_1923","tier":2,"tier_label":"near-primary recollection","url":"","what_it_does_not_supply":["any dimension","the extent of the ivy or creeper in any year","when the stable became a garage"],"what_it_supplies":["the roof covering as built: red baked tiles, unglazed","the street walls of Wellesley granite; all walls lined with hollow brick","the courtyard elevation of common brick of slightly pinkish colour with grey limestone trimmings, matched in the adjoining house's wall","the courtyard entrance from the alley, used on moving day in December 1887","Virginia creeper on the courtyard walls (carried from the old house, 1887) and Boston ivy on the street front (from plants sent after June 1887)","the service entrance 'through the great arch on Eighteenth Street'; the Eighteenth Street door and back stairs","outside iron-work Bower-Barffed rather than painted"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"Historic American Buildings Survey. John J. Glessner House (Now Chicago School of Architecture Foundation), 1800 Prairie Avenue. HABS No. ILL-1015. Photographs; Written Historical and Descriptive Data, data pages 1-23 (prepared by David T. Van Zanten, student assistant architect, and Osmund Overby, supervisory architect, August 1963) and addendum data pages 24-25 (inventory of photogrammetric images, 1985-1986). National Park Service; Library of Congress, Prints and Photographs Division.","source_id":"habs_glessner_house_il_1015_data_pages","tier":4,"tier_label":"later scholarship","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/il0118/","what_it_does_not_supply":["the widths of lots 38-40 in feet: the lot sizes of the Assessor's Division block are not stated","the date the stable became a garage, the date the roof tiles were tarred, or any other change between 1887 and 1946 that it does not list","measurements: the data pages describe; the drawings (habs_glessner_house_il_1015_drawings) measure"],"what_it_supplies":["the exterior construction as surveyed in 1963: foundations of Joliet limestone ending at the ground line; brick walls faced with alternately six- and eight-inch sheets of granite with deeper returns at openings; courtyard walls of light pink brick with Joliet limestone lintels and sills; steeply pitched gabled roofs covered with tar-covered terracotta tiles six inches wide, five inches exposed; copper sheathing on the roof of the entrance-hall bow","the original east carriage (porte-cochere) door as seen in a 1945 photograph: two heavily panelled leaves with heavy iron straps and latches","the list of 1946 alterations, which is the phase filter every 1963 dimension has to pass for 1904","the legal description and the March 1885 purchase of lots 39, 40 and the north 17 ft of lot 38"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"Exterior photograph of the John J. Glessner House, 1800 Prairie Avenue, from the north-east (Prairie Avenue and E. 18th Street), winter. The Inland Architect and News Record, vol. XI, no. 1 (February 1888), photograph section. Reproduced by Glessner House, 'Exterior from Inland Architect'.","source_id":"inland_architect_1888_02_glessner_exterior","tier":1,"tier_label":"period/eyewitness","url":"https://www.glessnerhouse.org/exterior-from-inland-architect","what_it_does_not_supply":["dimensions: it is an oblique photograph","colour","the courtyard or the alley side","the state of the vines in 1904 (in the photograph the walls are bare)"],"what_it_supplies":["the 1888 state of the Prairie Avenue facade and the north gable, bracketing the 1965 elevation","the 18th Street eave line and the west wing's north gable and ridge turret, which no HABS sheet draws","the absence of dormers on the Prairie Avenue roof slope"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"Wheeler, George H., Residence (1812 S. Prairie Ave.). Albert Levy, Architectural Photographic Series (New York), nos. 31, 33; photograph of the street front, c. 1885-1900. Ryerson & Burnham Art and Architecture Archives, Art Institute of Chicago, Special 779.4 L66a no. 31, 33.","source_id":"levy_1812_prairie_photo","tier":1,"tier_label":"period/eyewitness","url":"https://artic.contentdm.oclc.org/digital/collection/mqc/id/13492","what_it_does_not_supply":["colour: the print is monochrome, so every stone, brick and paint colour stays reconstructed","the side and rear walls, and the roof behind the gable except its two front corners","depth: how far the projection stands out is the 1911 sheet's"],"what_it_supplies":["the front's storey heights, the raised basement and the stair's rise, read at the front's plane","the south projection's width and height and its windows","the entrance's place on the north and the openings' places and sizes","the shaped gable's outline, its grid window and finial"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"Robinson, E. Robinson's Atlas of the City of Chicago. New York: E. Robinson, 1886. Plate 10, \"Part of 2.39.14 Assessors Div.\"","source_id":"robinson_1886_chicago_plate_10","tier":2,"tier_label":"near-primary recollection","url":"","what_it_does_not_supply":["Street widths: its bands are not drawn to the widths it prints (Prairie printed 66, drawn about 55 ft)","Anything as of 1904 on its own authority","Elevations or grades"],"what_it_supplies":["The water's edge beside the Illinois Central from above 16th Street to 18th, as of 1886 -- a bound, from before, on the 1904 edge","Lot and subdivision lines, and the outlines of the houses at 1609-1611 and 1620 Prairie that Glessner House names as standing in 1904 and gone by 1911"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"Sanborn Map Company. Insurance Maps of Chicago, Illinois, Volume 3. New York: Sanborn Map Company, 1911. Sheet 20.","source_id":"sanborn_1911_chicago_v3_sheet_20","tier":1,"tier_label":"period/eyewitness","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/sanborn01790_020/","what_it_does_not_supply":["Anything as of 1904 on its own authority. It is a 1911 survey; Glessner House's reading that everything on sheets 20, 28 and 35 but 1609-1611 and 1620 Prairie stood in 1904 is attributed guidance, recorded in that README, and not checked by this record","A construction date for any building it draws","Elevations, grades, curb heights or fill depths -- no Sanborn sheet carries a level","Architecture above the footprint beyond its story count, material colour and roof symbol","The lake edge beyond the stretch the sheet actually inks: the pen line stops while the tracks run on"],"what_it_supplies":["The Illinois Central / Michigan Central right-of-way and, from the 16th Street row south about 60 m, the water's edge beside it, as of 1911","Footprints, lot lines, street widths (66 ft; 16th Street 50 ft), addresses, material by colour and story counts for Indiana Av. to the tracks, 16th to 18th Street, as of 1911","Its own printed Scale of Feet, which the georeference uses as its scale"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"Sanborn Map Company. Insurance Maps of Chicago, Illinois, Volume 3. New York: Sanborn Map Company, 1911. Sheet 28.","source_id":"sanborn_1911_chicago_v3_sheet_28","tier":1,"tier_label":"period/eyewitness","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/sanborn01790_020/","what_it_does_not_supply":["Anything as of 1904 on its own authority. It is a 1911 survey; Glessner House's reading that everything on sheets 20, 28 and 35 but 1609-1611 and 1620 Prairie stood in 1904 is attributed guidance, recorded in that README, and not checked by this record","A construction date for any building it draws","Elevations, grades, curb heights or fill depths -- no Sanborn sheet carries a level","Architecture above the footprint beyond its story count, material colour and roof symbol","The lake edge beyond the stretch the sheet actually inks: the pen line stops while the tracks run on"],"what_it_supplies":["The water's edge beside the Illinois Central from about 40 m north of 18th Street to about 80 m south of it, and the easternmost track on to the paper's edge, as of 1911","The 1800 Prairie lot and the Glessner House footprint (stone, courtyard plan) as of 1911, and every Prairie- and Calumet-facing footprint from 18th to 20th Street","Its own printed Scale of Feet"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"Sanborn Map Company. Insurance Maps of Chicago, Illinois, Vol. 3 (1911), sheet 28: E. 18th St. to E. 20th St., Prairie Av. Blvd. and Calumet Av. to the Illinois Central / Michigan Central tracks and Lake Michigan. Scale 1\" = 50'. Stamped 'Fire Insurance Map Collection, Geography and Map Division, Library of Congress'; header 'Chicago Vol. 3', with 1891-edition cross-references (245-246) (252-253).","source_id":"sanborn_1911_vol3_sheet_28","tier":1,"tier_label":"period/eyewitness","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/sanborn01790_020/","what_it_does_not_supply":["anything about 1904 directly: a 1911 sheet shows only that a structure stood in 1911","the 1904 use of the west wing: '1911 GARAGE' cannot be backdated","architecture above the footprint - roof pitches, chimneys, openings","lot dimensions in figures for this lot: none are printed on it"],"what_it_supplies":["the house's position on its lot: north face on the E. 18th Street line, west face on the alley line, east face about 14.7 ft (±1.5 ft) behind the Prairie Avenue street line","a check on the HABS envelope: about 161 x 75 ft overall and a 35 x 59 ft west wing at 1\" = 50'","materials by colour (stone street walls, brick courtyard walls) and storeys (2B main house, 1½ west wing) in 1911","the neighbour that closes the courtyard on the south in 1911 (1808 Prairie and its rear garage)"]},{"archived_url":"","citation":"J. W. Taylor (Chicago), 'Kimball, William W., Residence (1801 S. Prairie Ave.)