PDF p. 10): the courtyard entrance from the alley, the Virginia creeper on the courtyard walls and the Boston ivy on the front; p. 15 (PDF p. 16): the service entrance through the great arch on Eighteenth Street.","sources":["glessner_story_of_a_house_1923","habs_glessner_house_il_1015_data_pages","inland_architect_1888_02_glessner_exterior"],"text":"Glessner House, 1800 Prairie stood on 1 July 1904, as its structure record carries it."},{"confidence":"inferred","id":"stood-2","reasoning":"STANDING IN 1904 is what the scene needs, and it rests on the 1911 Sanborn sheet 28 carried back seven years, the same reading the Glessner record and the 1904 parcels make of it (the Glessner House correspondent reads no change on this sheet between 1904 and 1911). FROM: the T-1837 building register reports 1886 (Cobb & Frost); it is not a source record here, so the day is the 1st of January and the confidence inferred. TO: the register reports the loss in 1968; same footing, the year's last day.","sources":["sanborn_1911_vol3_sheet_28","sanborn_1911_chicago_v3_sheet_28"],"text":"Osborn Keith House, 1808 Prairie stood on 1 July 1904, as its structure record carries it."},{"confidence":"inferred","id":"stood-3","reasoning":"The draft claims the 1904 target and no more: the 1911 sheet draws the building and the T-1840 census rules it present in 1904 (present_as_mapped). THE HOUSE'S DATE AND ITS MANSARD'S (T-2962): the house was 'completed in 1864' for Granville S. Ingraham (glessner_blog_2020_07_early_history_prairie_iv), and that post's author judges the mansard 'may well have been a later addition' to an Italianate house of the Civil War years; nothing read here dates the mansard, and the Keith house at 1900 Prairie had its mansard added in the 1880s (glessner_blog_2014_11_elbridge_keith_house), so a later mansard is the street's own pattern, not a proof for this house. For this phase the question is bounded either way: the c. 1890 photograph (earliest 1885) shows the mansard and the veranda front, and the 1911 sheet draws the mansard's curb and prints the house '3B', so the house as built here stood from at least the late 1880s to 1911. The range keeps to the 1904 target because only the front is seen in c. 1890; the flank's bays are the 1911 sheet's. When the house lost the mansard or was demolished is not read.","sources":["sanborn_1911_chicago_v3_sheet_20","glessner_blog_1736_mcbirney_c1890_photo","glessner_blog_2020_07_early_history_prairie_iv"],"text":"1736 Prairie stood on 1 July 1904, as its structure record carries it."},{"confidence":"inferred","id":"stood-4","reasoning":"Built for George H. Wheeler on the Real Estate and Building Journal's permit of June 7, 1884 ('3-story dwelling, 30x80', the Prairie library's npo-1812-rebj-1884-06-07-wheeler-dwelling-permit), photographed by Albert Levy c. 1885-1900 and drawn on the 1911 sheet ('3B'); the T-1841 census rules it present in 1904 (present_as_mapped). The range claims the 1904 target and the sheet's date and no more.","sources":["sanborn_1911_chicago_v3_sheet_28","levy_1812_prairie_photo"],"text":"Wheeler House, 1812 Prairie stood on 1 July 1904, as its structure record carries it."},{"confidence":"inferred","id":"stood-5","reasoning":"The 1904 target to the 1911 sheet that draws the house: the T-1841 census rules it present in 1904 (present_as_mapped), and the photograph (after 1873) shows the same plan. Its building and loss dates are not read here.","sources":["sanborn_1911_chicago_v3_sheet_28"],"text":"Henderson House, 1816 Prairie stood on 1 July 1904, as its structure record carries it."},{"confidence":"inferred","id":"stood-6","reasoning":"The draft claims the 1904 target and no more: the 1911 sheet draws the building and the T-1841 census rules it present in 1904 (present_as_mapped). Its building and loss dates are the per-building ticket's to read.","sources":["sanborn_1911_chicago_v3_sheet_28"],"text":"Coleman-Ames House, 1811 Prairie stood on 1 July 1904, as its structure record carries it."},{"confidence":"inferred","id":"stood-7","reasoning":"The record claims the 1904 target and no more: the 1911 sheet draws the building ('3B', stone) and the T-1841 census rules it present in 1904 (present_as_mapped); J. W. Taylor's photograph shows it standing about 1895. Its building and loss dates are the finishing ticket's to read (T-1890).","sources":["sanborn_1911_chicago_v3_sheet_28","taylor_c1895_kimball_residence_1801"],"text":"W. W. Kimball House, 1801 Prairie stood on 1 July 1904, as its structure record carries it."},{"confidence":"inferred","id":"stood-8","reasoning":"The draft claims the 1904 target and no more: the 1911 sheet draws the building and the T-1840 census rules it present in 1904 (present_as_mapped). Its building and loss dates are the per-building ticket's to read.","sources":["sanborn_1911_chicago_v3_sheet_20"],"text":"1726 Prairie stood on 1 July 1904, as its structure record carries it."},{"confidence":"inferred","id":"stood-9","reasoning":"The draft claims the 1904 target and no more: the 1911 sheet draws the building and the T-1840 census rules it present in 1904 (present_as_mapped). Its building and loss dates are the per-building ticket's to read.","sources":["sanborn_1911_chicago_v3_sheet_20"],"text":"1730 Prairie stood on 1 July 1904, as its structure record carries it."},{"confidence":"inferred","id":"stood-10","reasoning":"STANDING IN 1904 is what the scene needs, and it rests on sheet 28 of the 1911 Sanborn carried back seven years, the reading the Glessner and 1808 records and the T-1841 sheet census make of this sheet. FROM: the T-1837 building register dates the Wheeler house 1884, and the stable is read as built with it; the register is not a source record here and nothing dates the stable itself, so the day is the 1st of January and the confidence inferred. TO: the last day of the year the sheet was published, the latest date anything here shows the building standing; it is not a demolition date.","sou