-  "notes": "New house for Hugh J. (Johnston) McBirney, the son of Hugh McBirney of 1736: a narrow three-storey rock-faced stone front with a steep front gable (oculus), Tudor-style mullioned and transomed windows with stone quoined surrounds, a carved panel over a plain square-headed entrance, brick side wall with a mansard-roofed rear wing and dormers; attached at left to a neighbour with a corner turret, carved bay and Romanesque arched entrance; at right an open side yard with picket fence; building materials on the unfinished walk. ADDRESS: AIC catalogues this as 1736 S. Prairie, but 1736 (NW corner of 18th) was the elder McBirney's Second Empire house (blog c.1890 photo; Rand McNally 1893 strip), while the 1904 Blue Book lists the Hugh J. McBirney household at 1625 Prairie, and the 'Hibbardville' map places McBirney on the east side of the 1600 block between Farwell and Forsyth. The 1911 Sanborn shows 1625 as the end of an attached group 1621–1625 ('3B; section 4'), matching the attached-left/open-right siting here. Treat as 1625 (not in the building index) pending directory confirmation; it stood in 1904. Supporting context: Inland Architect Nov. 1885 (vol. 6 no. 5, building news) reports 'Architects Treat & Foltz: For Granger Farwell, at 1625 Prairie avenue, residence, cost $11,000' and the Conzen/Knox 1886 map places Farwell at 1623 — so the turreted, Romanesque-arched house attached at the LEFT (north) of the McBirney front is probably the 1885 Farwell house, and the McBirney house the next lot south (1625 by 1904), with the open lots toward 1637 at right.",
+  "notes": "New house for Hugh J. (Johnston) McBirney, the son of Hugh McBirney of 1736: a narrow three-storey rock-faced stone front with a steep front gable (oculus), Tudor-style mullioned and transomed windows with stone quoined surrounds, a carved panel over a plain square-headed entrance, brick side wall with a mansard-roofed rear wing and dormers; attached at left to a neighbour with a corner turret, carved bay and Romanesque arched entrance; at right an open side yard with picket fence; building materials on the unfinished walk. ADDRESS: AIC catalogues this as 1736 S. Prairie, but 1736 (NW corner of 18th) was the elder McBirney's Second Empire house (blog c.1890 photo; Rand McNally 1893 strip), while the 1904 Blue Book lists the Hugh J. McBirney household at 1625 Prairie, and the 'Hibbardville' map places McBirney on the east side of the 1600 block between Farwell and Forsyth. The 1911 Sanborn shows 1625 as the end of an attached group 1621–1625 ('3B; section 4'), matching the attached-left/open-right siting here. Treat as 1625 (not in the building index) pending directory confirmation; it stood in 1904. Supporting context: Inland Architect Nov. 1885 (vol. 6 no. 5, building news) reports 'Architects Treat & Foltz: For Granger Farwell, at 1625 Prairie avenue, residence, cost $11,000' and the Conzen/Knox 1886 map places Farwell at 1623 — so the turreted, Romanesque-arched house attached at the LEFT (north) of the McBirney front is probably the 1885 Farwell house, and the McBirney house the next lot south (1625 by 1904), with the open lots toward 1637 at right. T-3020 (2026-10-22): the AIC's '1736' is no longer listed under addresses. The library places a record on every lot its addresses name, so that wrong catalogue address put this plate on 1736's lot, at the top of 1736's card, where the owner saw the son's house standing in for the father's. The plate is 1625's: Chicagology prints the same photograph on its 1625 page (cgn-1625-mcbirney-inland-1890-copy) and the sheet 20 census (T-1840) seats it there.",
